WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
Deep RL.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:06.200
It breaks in characteristic ways, and knowing them is a professional skill.

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.667
The centerpiece is the deadly triad.

4
00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.867
Bootstrapping: targets built from your own estimates.

5
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:16.033
Off-policy data: replay from old behavior.

6
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:20.933
And function approximation: networks that generalize errors across states.

7
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:22.575
Each is fine alone.

8
00:00:22.575 --> 00:00:26.033
Together, value estimates can spiral to infinity.

9
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.733
DQN sits squarely on all three corners.

10
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:35.300
Its replay buffer and target network are engineering duct tape holding the triad together.

11
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.633
Beyond the triad, three more failures.

12
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:43.500
Sample inefficiency: millions of steps for tasks a human learns in minutes.

13
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.867
Reward hacking: the agent optimizes the letter of the reward, never its spirit.

14
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:53.867
And seed sensitivity: the same code can succeed or fail on different seeds.

15
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:56.633
Here is what that looks like.

16
00:00:56.933 --> 00:01:01.300
Same code, same hyperparameters, different seeds, very different curves.

17
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.667
A single learning curve is an anecdote, not a result.

18
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:07.767
Here we remove DQN's anchor.

19
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:12.735
Setting the target update interval to one makes the target the online net.

20
00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:15.700
Watch the q-values balloon and reward collapse.

21
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.623
Then never trust one seed.

22
00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:19.933
Run five, and report mean and spread.

23
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:26.633
A vendor claims RL optimizes satellite downlinks forty percent better than heuristics.

24
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:30.567
One environment, one learning curve, no seeds, no baseline.

25
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:37.900
You now have three questions: how many seeds, what does the simple baseline score, and what loophole might the reward have?

26
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:39.904
Let's recap.

27
00:01:39.904 --> 00:01:45.200
The deadly triad is bootstrapping, off-policy data, and function approximation.

28
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.733
Expect inefficiency, reward hacking, and seed noise.

29
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:53.390
Run five or more seeds, and prefer the simplest thing that works.

30
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.200
Next up: Stable Baselines3.
